        <mxCell id="sdmH2Lh8s6HiHu3jjXA6-362" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=hidden;rounded=0;" value="&lt;h1 style=&quot;margin-top: 0px;&quot;&gt;3. MetaSpace&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;This special type of memory which is not part of heap memory neither JVM.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;This part of Native OS memory.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;This will be used to store&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;Class structure, templates&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Class metadata which will be referenced from heap memory&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Method bytecode.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Annotations and class file attributes&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Runtime constant pool i.e. symbolic references, method/field descriptions.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;Before java8, it was part of heap memory and was called as PemGen, which was having fixed memory, which once full throw error i.e. &lt;b&gt;OutOfMemoryError&amp;nbsp;&lt;/b&gt;&amp;nbsp;as when multiple classes loads&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;With Introduction of metaspace, memory became expandable and pure read-mostly class metadata&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;b&gt;Internal Architecture &amp;amp; Sizing&lt;br&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;MetaSpace is managed via chunk allocators tied to classloaders&lt;/li&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;When &lt;b&gt;ClassLoaader &lt;/b&gt;loads classes, the JVM allocates chucks of native memory specifically for that loader.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;b&gt;Garbage Collection:&lt;/b&gt; Metadata in Metaspace is freed &lt;b&gt;only when its defining &lt;code&gt;ClassLoader&lt;/code&gt; itself becomes unreachable and is garbage collected&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;sup class=&quot;superscript&quot;&gt;&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;. Individual classes loaded by standard system loaders are practically never unloaded.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;h3&gt;Diagnosing Common Metaspace Issues&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;If your application throws,&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;It usually indicates a &lt;b&gt;ClassLoader leak&lt;/b&gt;, caused by:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;ol start=&quot;1&quot;&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Dynamic Bytecode Generation:&lt;/b&gt; Excessive runtime proxy generation without caching (e.g., unconstrained CGLIB, ByteBuddy, reflection, or dynamic scripting engines like Groovy/Nashorn).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;App Server Redeployments:&lt;/b&gt; Failing to unregister static thread locals, JDBC drivers, or background threads during hot redeployments, preventing the previous application ClassLoader from ever being garbage collected.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="490" width="1100" x="20" y="5660" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="sdmH2Lh8s6HiHu3jjXA6-364" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=hidden;rounded=0;" value="&lt;h1 style=&quot;margin-top: 0px;&quot;&gt;Garbage Collection&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;Garbage Collection (GC) is the JVM&#39;s automatic memory management process that identifies and reclaims memory allocated on the Heap&amp;nbsp; that is no longer reachable by application.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;GC runs of Algo called &lt;b&gt;Mark &amp;amp; sweep.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;In this algo, When GC runs,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;Mark Process run where it will mark all object which are ready to be removed from the memory&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;When Sweeps run, it will do two thing, first it will remove the objects which were marked earlier and second it will move the surviving object further part of heap memory which will be explained in below section.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="160" width="1080" x="40" y="6160" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="sdmH2Lh8s6HiHu3jjXA6-365" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=#82b366;fillColor=#d5e8d4;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;gradientColor=#97d077;" value="&lt;b&gt;How Object is created and managed in Heap&lt;/b&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="30" width="420" x="265" y="6360" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="sdmH2Lh8s6HiHu3jjXA6-366" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#fa6800;fontColor=#000000;strokeColor=#C73500;" value="Young Generation" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="60" width="240" x="460" y="6480" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="sdmH2Lh8s6HiHu3jjXA6-367" parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#647687;fontColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#314354;" value="Old generation&amp;nbsp;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="60" width="240" x="700" y="6480" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
